window.QF_QA=[{"t":"Home","h":"Quantock Financial: Mortgages made easy","u":"/#quantock-financial-mortgages-made-easy","p":"index.html","x":"Quantock Financial: Mortgages made easy We offer whole of market, bespoke mortgage advice for first charge mortgages to suit a wide range of clients. We can help with a residential purchase, remortgage, first-time buyers, buy-to-let, house in multiple occupation (HMO) and holiday properties. Our service ensures the right mortgage for you. Choosing a mortgage can be daunting. A product with the lowest rate isn’t always the best deal. Additional arrangement fees or a valuation to pay can make a mortgage much more expensive than one with a higher rate that has no fees. We consider all the options to find the right deal for you. Based in Taunton, we offer experienced and professional, whole of market advice for first charge mortgages and mortgage protection specific to your situation or circumstances. We guide you through the process and offer bespoke protection solutions using our knowledge & experience. For expert, professional mortgage advice call Quantock Financial on 01823 426046 to book your initial FEE FREE consultation. We can secure exclusive deals that are only available to brokers and not to the general public. Our service includes meetings in person or via Microsoft Teams / Zoom, telephone advice, researching the whole of the market for first charge mortgages, obtaining an Agreement in Principle (AIP), submitting your mortgage application and following it through to offer with regular updates. We also liaise with Solicitors, Estate Agents and Valuers on your behalf. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. Not all Buy to Let mortgages are regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority."},{"t":"Home","h":"How can we help?","u":"/#enquiry-form","p":"index.html","x":"Online enquiry form How can we help? Choose an option, answer a few short questions and an adviser will be in touch."},{"t":"Home","h":"Finding the right mortgage for you","u":"/#finding-the-right-mortgage-for-you","p":"index.html","x":"Mortgages Finding the right mortgage for you Choosing a mortgage is about more than just the interest rate. It’s about finding the product that best fits your unique needs and goals. Thanks to our panel of lenders, we can offer you access to a broad range of mortgage products, including exclusive deals that are only available through brokers like us, not directly from lenders."},{"t":"Home","h":"Protecting the ones that matter","u":"/#protecting-the-ones-that-matter","p":"index.html","x":"Protection Protecting the ones that matter At Quantock Financial, we understand that personal protection is about securing the future of those who depend on you financially. The aim of financial protection is to ensure that, in the event of your death or serious illness, your loved ones are provided with the financial support they need. It’s important to consider the potential impact and consequences your absence or ill health could have on those closest to you. By planning ahead with the right protection solutions, you can safeguard your family or dependants from financial uncertainty, ensuring that they are taken care of during life’s most challenging times."},{"t":"Home","h":"Giving you peace of mind","u":"/#giving-you-peace-of-mind","p":"index.html","x":"Insurance Giving you peace of mind Insurance is key to securing your financial future and protecting loved ones. Life insurance provides a lump sum or income upon death to cover debts or living expenses. Critical illness cover offers financial support if diagnosed with a serious condition, while Family Income Benefit provides ongoing payments for dependants. Income protection safeguards your earnings if you can’t work due to illness or injury, and buildings and contents insurance shields your home and belongings from theft or natural disasters."},{"t":"Home","h":"Supporting NHS heroes with tailored financial solutions","u":"/#supporting-nhs-heroes-with-tailored-financial-so","p":"index.html","x":"Supporting our heroes Supporting NHS heroes with tailored financial solutions At Quantock Financial, we proudly support NHS employees by offering tailored financial services through Health Staff Discounts. We work closely with NHS staff across the country, understanding the unique challenges and opportunities they face. Our goal is to provide expert financial advice and solutions that help them secure a brighter financial future, whether it’s through personal protection, savings, or retirement planning. We’re here to ensure that those who take care of others are well taken care of themselves."},{"t":"Home","h":"Mortgage Calculator","u":"/#calculator","p":"index.html","x":"Mortgage Calculator A quick and easy way to calculate your monthly mortgage payments. Simply enter the amount you wish to borrow, the interest rate and the term over which you intend to pay it off and. Then hit the ‘Calculate’ button. We have extensive mortgage and financial services experience and put our clients’ needs first. Offering a comprehensive choice of mortgages, many that can’t be found on the high street. All our advice is tailor-made to our clients’ requirements. This information is a guide only and should not be relied on as a recommendation or advice that any particular mortgage is suitable for you. All mortgages are subject to the applicant(s) meeting the eligibility criteria of lenders. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. How much could I borrow? Try our borrowing calculator"},{"t":"Home","h":"News, Views & Advice","u":"/#news-views-advice","p":"index.html","x":"Quantock Financial News, Views & Advice Newsletter Navigating the mortgage market Shared ownership: Your guide to homebuying on a budget Protection What is critical illness cover? Mortgage First-time buyers"},{"t":"Home","h":"Client Testimonials","u":"/#client-testimonials","p":"index.html","x":"Client Testimonials What our clients have to say Neil provided an excellent and prompt service throughout, getting me a mortgage deal I was very happy with. As a first time buyer, he helped me through the process and deal with numerous frustrating setbacks along the way. Highly recommended. D Haughey QF, Will Hudson was a reliable mortgage advisor. I would like to express my gratitude for the support and invaluable advice you’ve provided throughout the process. I really admire the professionalism and persistence you’ve shown. A De Silva Neil has helped me to get my first mortgage in 2020 and more recently has helped me to re-mortgage. He is extremely knowledgeable and is always quick to respond and help when needed. I have been very impressed with his service and would highly recommend. H Wood Neil was proficient and patient. He took the time to explain terminology fully to me, as a first time buyer. Neil found a mortgage deal for me that other brokers were not able to. He fully addressed concerns in emails. Neil was also able to advise on mortgage protection and produced multiple quotes to allow me to weigh up the best option for me. Buying a house is stressful but Neil made this aspect much, much smoother! A Gupta Wonderful service, so friendly and helpful. Would highly recommend to anyone. Neil Hudson helped me and my partner with our first home and I couldn’t have asked for a better service. Neil was very patient, reliable, approachable and always available whenever we needed him. D Sellick Read our Google reviews"},{"t":"Mortgages Overview","h":"Mortgage Advice","u":"/mortgages#mortgage-advice","p":"mortgages.html","x":"Mortgage Advice Finding the right mortgage for you Choosing a mortgage is about more than just the interest rate. It’s about finding the product that best fits your unique needs and goals. We understand how important it is to secure the right property. Whether you’re buying your first home, looking to reduce your monthly payments, or expanding your property portfolio, we’re here to make the process as simple, affordable, and stress-free as possible. We’ll guide you through every step of your mortgage journey, from making informed decisions to submitting your application and beyond. Thanks to our panel of lenders, we can offer you access to a broad range of mortgage products, including exclusive deals that are only available through brokers like us, not directly from lenders. Please remember: Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"Mortgages Overview","h":"How do I repay my mortgage?","u":"/mortgages#how-do-i-repay-my-mortgage","p":"mortgages.html","x":"How do I repay my mortgage? Mortgages should be straightforward: you borrow money to buy a home and pay interest on the loan. With a highly competitive market, banks and building societies are constantly updating and expanding their range of mortgage options. The key points to consider are how you’ll repay the capital you borrow and how you’ll handle the interest. Repayment mortgages vs. interest-only mortgages There are two main ways to repay the capital on your mortgage: Repayment Mortgage With a repayment mortgage, each monthly payment goes towards both the capital and the interest on the loan. By the end of the mortgage term, if all payments have been made on time, the mortgage will be fully paid off. Interest-Only Mortgage With an interest-only mortgage, you only pay the interest on the loan each month. At the end of the mortgage term, you will need to repay the full amount of the capital. This is often done through a separate investment plan."},{"t":"Buy-to-Let Mortgages","h":"Planning","u":"/mortgages-buy-to-let#planning","p":"mortgages-buy-to-let.html","x":"Planning If you’re planning to buy a property to rent out, you’ll need a buy-to-let mortgage. As many landlords know, the market has changed a lot in recent years, but there are still plenty of great mortgage options available. Whether you’re an experienced landlord with a portfolio of properties or you’re investing in your very first buy-to-let, property can be a valuable long-term asset. We can help you find a competitive buy-to-let mortgage from a wide range of products across the market, whether you’re purchasing a new investment property or remortgaging an existing one. If you’re considering buying to let, securing the right mortgage is one of the most important steps. You won’t be able to use a standard residential mortgage. You’ll need a specialist buy-to-let mortgage designed specifically for landlords, and that’s where we come in."},{"t":"Buy-to-Let Mortgages","h":"Rates","u":"/mortgages-buy-to-let#rates","p":"mortgages-buy-to-let.html","x":"Rates Buy-to-let mortgage rates vary and are dependent on the risk of the mortgage to the lender as well as the deposit available for an individual to put down. Buy-to-let mortgage rates are often higher than residential rates. With a buy-to-let loan, mortgage lenders will look at the expected rental income and some may require a minimum earned income too. You can usually choose between a range of mortgage deals, including fixed rate and tracker loans. Arrangement fees also apply and they can be high. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. Not all Buy to Let mortgages are regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority."},{"t":"Moving House","h":"Moving House","u":"/mortgages-moving-house#moving-house","p":"mortgages-moving-house.html","x":"Moving House Moving home? Let us help you find the right mortgage Whether you’re moving to a bigger property or downsizing, we’re here to help you find the mortgage that best suits your new needs. In some cases, the right deal might even be with your current lender! The mortgage market changes daily, with new products being released, including some that aren’t available directly on the high street. This gives you the opportunity to “lock in” a great deal tailored to your budget and requirements. At Quantock Financial Services, we have access to exclusive deals from many leading high street lenders, giving you even more choice. Remember: A mortgage is a loan secured against your home. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"First-Time Buyer Mortgage","h":"First-Time Buyer","u":"/mortgages-first-time-buyer#first-time-buyer","p":"mortgages-first-time-buyer.html","x":"First-Time Buyer Your guide to getting a mortgage for your first home If you’re planning to buy your first home, you’ll likely need a mortgage: a loan that’s secured against the value of the property. Securing a mortgage for your first home can sometimes feel overwhelming, with lots of decisions to make along the way. That’s where we’re here to help. Get your finances in shape The size of your mortgage will depend partly on the deposit you’re able to put down. Generally, the bigger your deposit, the better the mortgage deal you’ll be able to secure, which can help lower your monthly repayments. Get an Agreement in Principle We can help you apply for an Agreement in Principle (AIP) from a suitable lender. An AIP is a confirmation that a lender would be willing to consider your mortgage application and indicates the amount they may be prepared to lend. Having an AIP can give you an advantage when making an offer on a property, showing sellers and estate agents that you’re serious and ready to proceed. You can complete your full mortgage application once you’ve had an offer accepted. Find the right mortgage deal As a first-time buyer, it’s important to find a mortgage that suits your individual needs. We can talk you through a wide range of products from across the market, from fixed-rate and variable-rate deals to capped rates and offset mortgages. If affordability is a concern, you may also be eligible for help through a government home ownership scheme. We’ll explore all your options with you. The key is finding a mortgage that’s right for you, and we’ll make sure you do. Complete your application When you’re ready, we’ll guide you through the full mortgage application process, including providing details about the property and the mortgage you’ve chosen. Important: Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"Later Life Lending","h":"Why do I need a Lifetime Mortgage?","u":"/mortgages-later-life#why-do-i-need-a-lifetime-mortgage","p":"mortgages-later-life.html","x":"Why do I need a Lifetime Mortgage? Is a Lifetime Mortgage right for you? A Lifetime Mortgage isn’t the right choice for everyone, and our qualified advisers are here to help you find out if it’s the right fit for your situation. Many people use equity release to fund home improvements, make essential repairs, or simply enjoy a better lifestyle, whether that’s taking a dream holiday or helping family financially. Whatever your goals, we’re here to guide you through your options. How the process works Because Lifetime Mortgages can be complex and there are many plans available, the first step is to have a conversation with one of our fully qualified advisers. We’ll take the time to understand your circumstances and what you’re hoping to achieve, either in the comfort of your own home or over the phone, whichever suits you best. We’ll only recommend a Lifetime Mortgage if it’s truly the right choice for you. If it isn’t, we’ll tell you so and help you explore other options. Every plan we recommend is approved by the Equity Release Council and includes important protections, such as the No Negative Equity Guarantee , meaning you’ll never owe more than the value of your home. The legal side As part of the process, you’ll need a solicitor to provide independent legal advice and ensure you fully understand the terms of the Lifetime Mortgage. While we’ll handle the financial advice, your appointed solicitor will look after the legal aspects to protect your interests. Things to consider A Lifetime Mortgage may not be suitable for everyone and could affect your entitlement to means-tested benefits. It’s essential to seek advice before making any decisions and to carefully consider all available options. Keep in mind that the interest on a Lifetime Mortgage is added to both the original loan and the accumulated interest, meaning the amount you owe can grow over time. This could reduce the remaining equity in your home and the value of any inheritance you plan to leave. We encourage you to involve your family or beneficiaries in the process, as a Lifetime Mortgage may impact their future inheritance. They are welcome to join any meetings with your financial adviser to ask questions and share in the decision-making. Find out more For more information, please contact us: Phone: 01823 426046 Email: office@quantockfinancial.co.uk"},{"t":"Remortgaging","h":"Remortgage","u":"/mortgages-remortgage#remortgage","p":"mortgages-remortgage.html","x":"Remortgage Thinking of remortgaging? We can help you find the right deal Remortgaging means switching your current mortgage to a new deal with a different lender, without having to move. Many homeowners choose to remortgage to take advantage of better interest rates or to reduce monthly repayments. However, remortgaging isn’t just about saving money; it could also be a way to gain more flexibility or raise additional funds. There are several reasons you might decide to remortgage: To save money by switching to a more competitive deal To consolidate existing debt or raise funds for other purposes To finance home improvements If you’re considering switching your mortgage, it’s important to think about the overall repayment period too. While your monthly payments might be lower, the total length of your mortgage could be longer than your current deal, so it’s worth checking the final repayment date. Important: Think carefully before securing other debts against your property. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"Giving you peace of mind","h":"Insurance","u":"/insurance#insurance","p":"insurance.html","x":"Insurance Insurance is an important part of securing your financial future and taking care of the people you love. Life insurance can provide a lump sum or regular payments to help cover debts, living costs, or support your family if you pass away. Critical illness cover gives you financial help if you’re diagnosed with a serious condition, while Family Income Benefit offers ongoing payments to support your dependants. Income protection covers part of your earnings if you’re unable to work because of illness or injury, and buildings and contents insurance protects your home and belongings against theft, damage, or natural events."},{"t":"Life Insurance","h":"Life Insurance","u":"/insurance-life#life-insurance","p":"insurance-life.html","x":"Life Insurance Getting the right life insurance in place can be life changing, offering real peace of mind for you and financial security for your loved ones. Life insurance provides either a lump sum or a regular income if you pass away, creating a vital financial safety net for your family. This payout can help clear debts, like an outstanding mortgage, or cover everyday living expenses. Although it’s not easy to think about a time when you’re no longer here, protecting the things that matter (your family, your home, and their future) is incredibly important. Life insurance can offer real reassurance. It’s quick and simple to arrange, and it’s often more affordable than you might think. By paying a monthly premium, your family could receive a cash payout if you die during the policy term. In some cases, a payout could also be made early if you’re diagnosed with a terminal illness (depending on your policy terms). We know it can sometimes feel confusing, especially because everyone’s protection needs are different. That’s why it’s important to find the right cover for you. Whether you want to cover your family’s mortgage or make sure your loved ones are financially supported in the future, we’re here to help you choose the right protection for your needs."},{"t":"Buildings and Contents","h":"Helping you to protect and take care of your home","u":"/insurance-buildings-contents#helping-you-to-protect-and-take-care-of-your-hom","p":"insurance-buildings-contents.html","x":"Helping you to protect and take care of your home Having the right buildings and contents insurance in place means your home and possessions are covered in case of events like theft, fire, or natural disasters like flooding. You can choose to buy buildings and contents insurance separately or as a combined policy, depending on your needs. Homes are more than just structures. They’re full of memories and personal items. That’s why everyone’s insurance needs are unique. We’re here to help, whether you’re looking to set up new coverage, tailor your existing policy to fit your needs, or even see if we can help lower your current premiums. The price of insurance can vary depending on things like whether accidental damage or home emergency cover is included. Get in touch for a no-obligation consultation. We’ll walk you through the options to ensure you find the right cover that suits both your budget and your needs."},{"t":"Business Protection","h":"Protecting the business you have built","u":"/insurance-business-protection#protecting-the-business-you-have-built","p":"insurance-business-protection.html","x":"Protecting the business you have built Most businesses depend on a small number of people. If an owner, director or key member of staff died or became seriously ill, the business could lose income, struggle to repay what it has borrowed, or be left unsure who owns it. Business protection is insurance that is designed to help a business carry on if that happens. We can talk you through the options and help you find cover that suits your business."},{"t":"Business Protection","h":"Who is business protection for?","u":"/insurance-business-protection#who-is-business-protection-for","p":"insurance-business-protection.html","x":"Who is business protection for? It can be worth looking at whatever the size of your business, including: Sole traders and partnerships Directors and shareholders of limited companies Businesses that rely on one or two key people Businesses with loans, overdrafts or directors' loans"},{"t":"Business Protection","h":"Types of business protection","u":"/insurance-business-protection#types-of-business-protection","p":"insurance-business-protection.html","x":"Types of business protection Key person cover Pays the business a lump sum if a named key person dies or, if you choose to include it, is diagnosed with a specified critical illness during the term of the policy. The money can help to replace lost profits or cover the cost of finding someone new. Shareholder and partnership protection Provides money so that the remaining owners can buy the share of an owner who dies or, if you choose to include it, becomes critically ill. The business stays with the people running it and the owner's family receives the value of their share. Business loan protection Helps the business to repay its borrowing, such as a loan, overdraft or commercial mortgage, if an owner or key person who the lending depends on dies or becomes critically ill. Relevant life cover A life insurance policy that a company takes out for a director or employee. It pays a lump sum to their family if they die while employed by the business. Executive income protection A policy paid for by the business that provides a regular income if a director or employee is unable to work because of illness or injury."},{"t":"Business Protection","h":"How we can help","u":"/insurance-business-protection#how-we-can-help","p":"insurance-business-protection.html","x":"How we can help We will take the time to understand how your business is set up, who it depends on and what it owes. We will then explain the cover available and recommend what we believe is right for you. The tax treatment of business protection depends on the circumstances of the business and the people covered, and may change in the future. We recommend that you also speak to your accountant."},{"t":"Protection","h":"Personal Protection","u":"/protection#personal-protection","p":"protection.html","x":"Personal Protection Personal protection: securing the future of those who depend on you Personal protection is designed to provide financial support for the people who rely on you. It’s important to think about the impact your death or a serious illness could have on your loved ones. Taking steps now can help ensure they are financially supported during a difficult time."},{"t":"Protection","h":"Critical Illness Cover","u":"/protection#critical-illness-cover","p":"protection.html","x":"Critical Illness Cover For many, the financial risks of a critical illness or injury are more important (and likely) than death, as it may lead to loss of work, treatment and additional care costs. A critical illness policy will make a lump sum payment or provide a regular income (see below under Family Income Benefit) on diagnosis of pre-defined “critical” illnesses, serious conditions or injuries. The conditions covered and level of pay-out vary greatly from policy to policy so it is vital to read the terms and conditions of each policy rather than simply opting for the cheapest one."},{"t":"Critical Illness Cover","h":"Critical Illness","u":"/protection-critical-illness#critical-illness","p":"protection-critical-illness.html","x":"Critical Illness Critical Illness Cover: protecting you when life takes an unexpected turn Critical Illness Cover (also known as Critical Illness Insurance) can help protect your finances if you’re diagnosed with a serious illness. This long-term insurance policy covers specific critical illnesses listed in the terms and conditions. If you’re diagnosed with one of these conditions, the policy will provide either a lump sum or regular income to help cover your mortgage or other expenses during a challenging time. Examples of conditions that may be covered include: Stroke Heart attack Certain types and stages of cancer Conditions like multiple sclerosis Most policies also cover permanent disabilities resulting from illness or injury. With most policies, the payout is a one-time payment and the policy ends after the payout. Some policies may offer a smaller payment for less severe conditions or if one of your children is diagnosed with one of the covered illnesses. It’s important to note that Critical Illness Cover will only pay out if you’re diagnosed with one of the conditions listed in the policy, and each condition may have specific criteria that need to be met for a payout."},{"t":"Family Income Benefit","h":"Family Income Benefit","u":"/protection-family-income-benefit#family-income-benefit","p":"protection-family-income-benefit.html","x":"Family Income Benefit can help maintain your family’s financial stability if the worst should happen to you. If you pass away or suffer a specified critical illness, a Family Income Benefit policy is designed to provide regular monthly payments until the end of the policy term. This helps replace the income you would have provided, ensuring your loved ones are supported. Rather than a lump sum, the policy pays out a monthly income to help cover ongoing expenses, such as childcare and school fees, as your family adjusts to the changes. Typically, premiums are paid monthly, and at the end of the policy term, there is no cash value. If you choose a policy with ‘Guaranteed Premiums,’ the amount you pay each month is locked in and will not increase throughout the policy term."},{"t":"Income Protection Cover","h":"Income Protection Cover","u":"/protection-income-protection#income-protection-cover","p":"protection-income-protection.html","x":"Income Protection Cover ensures your income is protected if illness or injury prevents you from working. When your income is crucial, it’s worth taking steps to safeguard it. If you’re unable to work due to illness or injury, Income Protection Cover pays you a regular income. The payments begin after an agreed waiting period, known as the deferred period . The deferred period is the time between your first day off work and when your benefit payments begin. Choosing a longer deferred period typically means lower premiums. For example, if your employer provides sick pay for a few months, you may be able to select a longer deferred period to keep costs down. We know that everyone’s protection needs are different, and it’s important to choose the right cover for your situation. Income Protection Cover offers peace of mind, knowing that you won’t lose your earnings if health issues or an accident keep you from working."},{"t":"About Us","h":"About Quantock Financial","u":"/about#about-quantock-financial","p":"about.html","x":"About Quantock Financial Quantock Financial Services helps people make the best financial decisions possible. We devote our time to you and your situation, making your financial experience as stress free and as beneficial to you as we can. Our clients are offered a traditional bespoke service as well as the assurance that we are giving you the best possible advice, as we work to achieve the best results for each client and their specific situation. As simple as possible We like to keep things as simple as possible for you and always act with integrity, keeping your requirements at the heart of everything we do. We offer open and honest advice and explain the terms, costs, value and significance of all products recommended. We like to work ‘paperlessly’ and online / over the phone with clients wherever possible. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"About Us","h":"Neil Hudson BSc Cert II (MP)","u":"/about#neil-hudson","p":"about.html","x":"Neil Hudson BSc Cert II (MP) Director & Mortgage & Protection Adviser M: 07918 612847 T: 01823 426046 Neil has a natural ability to understand his clients and relate to their situations. This, combined with his passion for all things property enables him to help all types of client. He is able to think outside the box and consider all possibilities in interesting property scenarios. He has built a very strong relationship with many of his clients, which is down to his hard work, honesty and sound advice. Outside of work he lives on the Quantock Hills, enjoys walking and the countryside with family and friends. He is keen to support local businesses on the Quantocks and in the Taunton area."},{"t":"About Us","h":"William Hudson Cert II (MP)","u":"/about#william-hudson","p":"about.html","x":"William Hudson Cert II (MP) Mortgage & Protection Adviser M: 07728 289908 T: 01823 426046 Will is a caring and diligent mortgage and protection adviser who assists clients with their varying mortgage needs. He is able to help all clients whatever their situation, but enjoys helping first-time buyers. As a younger adviser, he knows and understands the struggles and strains first-time buyers go through, but is always there to help and assist clients through the process, taking their stress away. Will is also passionate about protecting his clients, ensuring they can stay in their home should the unexpected happen. Away from work Will enjoys travelling and often undertakes European trips to ski and soak up the culture. He is also fond of The Quantocks and all they have to offer, and is often involved in countryside sports and activities."},{"t":"About Us","h":"Claire Ratcliffe Cert II (MP&ER)","u":"/about#claire","p":"about.html","x":"Claire Ratcliffe Cert II (MP&ER) Mortgage & Protection Adviser M: 07447 026047 T: 01823 426046 Claire is a kind and attentive Mortgage & Protection Adviser who takes the time to get to know her clients properly. She believes that is how the right advice is found: a mortgage that is suitable and affordable, and the reassurance of being protected financially should the unexpected happen. Originally from Australia, Claire has made the UK her permanent home. Away from work she loves exploring its history, one castle or manor house at a time."},{"t":"About Us","h":"Hattie Rogers CeMAP","u":"/about#hattie","p":"about.html","x":"Hattie Rogers CeMAP Mortgage & Protection Adviser M: 07467 879882 T: 01823 426046 Hattie is a Mortgage & Protection Adviser with a passion for helping people achieve their property goals while protecting what matters most. She believes that great advice starts with understanding the person behind the application. Whether you're buying your first home, moving house, remortgaging, investing in property, or reviewing your protection arrangements, Hattie takes the time to understand your goals and provide clear, tailored advice every step of the way. With a straightforward and approachable style, she aims to make what can often feel like a complex process as simple and stress-free as possible. Outside of work, Hattie loves travelling, exploring new places, and making the most of the outdoors. For her, it's about more than arranging a mortgage or insurance policy; it's about helping clients build financial confidence and make informed decisions for their future."},{"t":"What we do","h":"Mortgage Advice","u":"/about-what-we-do#mortgage-advice","p":"about-what-we-do.html","x":"Mortgage Advice Choosing a mortgage can be daunting. A product with the lowest rate isn’t always the right deal. Additional arrangement fees or a valuation to pay can make a mortgage much more expensive than one with a higher rate that has no fees. We consider all the options to find the right deal for you. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"What we do","h":"Protection Strategy","u":"/about-what-we-do#protection-strategy","p":"about-what-we-do.html","x":"Protection Strategy We provide a reliable protection strategy that will give you or your family an umbrella of cover in case of death or disability of either a family member or a key person in a business. This protection strategy will provide funds that will help handle family or business concerns."},{"t":"What we do","h":"Our Service","u":"/about-what-we-do#our-service","p":"about-what-we-do.html","x":"Our Service Our service includes meetings in person or via Microsoft Teams / Zoom, telephone advice, researching an extensive panel of lenders, obtaining an Agreement in Principle (AIP), submitting your mortgage application and following it through to offer with regular updates. We also liaise with Solicitors, Estate Agents and Valuers on your behalf."},{"t":"What we do","h":"How we work","u":"/about-what-we-do#how-we-work","p":"about-what-we-do.html","x":"How we work Getting to know you We want to get to know you and your family to find out your exact needs. That way we can give you the best mortgage and protection advice. We can advise you around the home buying process, the different types of surveys available and information and help regarding solicitors if needed. Finding you the right mortgage When it comes to finding you the right mortgage we have access to thousands of mortgage deals from the whole of the market for first charge mortgages including exclusive deals only available to brokers. Using our expert knowledge, we will find the right mortgage that will work for you. Getting you the right deal based on your circumstances. Protecting you and your family As part of our advice process, we will also look at protecting you and your family. This will ensure that should the worst happen you will have peace of mind that you have the best protection in place to help you stay in your home. Ongoing Support We will be there for you every step of the way to ensure a smooth completion. We will be on hand for any questions. Even after the mortgage has completed we will be in touch to review your mortgage and protection to ensure they are still suitable for you as time goes by. We are here to help you!"},{"t":"Refer a friend","h":"Refer a friend","u":"/about-refer-a-friend#refer-a-friend","p":"about-refer-a-friend.html","x":"Refer a friend Refer your friends and family and receive a thank you gift from us! We grow our business by word of mouth or referrals and to those clients who make a referral, we are very grateful. For each new client that you refer to us, if their mortgage completes, we can pay you a referral reward of £50 (with a maximum of 10 referrals over a 12 month period). Terms and conditions apply. However, you do not have to be an existing client of Quantock Financial Services to refer a new client to us. To be eligible for payments under our referral scheme, no purchase of any goods or services by the referee is necessary. Simply refer and be rewarded!"},{"t":"Client Testimonials","h":"Client Testimonials","u":"/about-client-testimonials#client-testimonials","p":"about-client-testimonials.html","x":"Neil provided an excellent and prompt service throughout, getting me a mortgage deal I was very happy with. As a first time buyer, he helped me through the process and deal with numerous frustrating setbacks along the way. Highly recommended. D Haughey QF, Will Hudson was a reliable mortgage advisor. I would like to express my gratitude for the support and invaluable advice you’ve provided throughout the process. I really admire the professionalism and persistence you’ve shown. A De Silva Neil has helped me to get my first mortgage in 2020 and more recently has helped me to re-mortgage. He is extremely knowledgeable and is always quick to respond and help when needed. I have been very impressed with his service and would highly recommend. H Wood Neil was proficient and patient. He took the time to explain terminology fully to me, as a first time buyer. Neil found a mortgage deal for me that other brokers were not able to. He fully addressed concerns in emails. Neil was also able to advise on mortgage protection and produced multiple quotes to allow me to weigh up the best option for me. Buying a house is stressful but Neil made this aspect much, much smoother! A Gupta Wonderful service, so friendly and helpful. Would highly recommend to anyone. Neil Hudson helped me and my partner with our first home and I couldn’t have asked for a better service. Neil was very patient, reliable, approachable and always available whenever we needed him. D Sellick Read our Google reviews"},{"t":"Free Credit Report","h":"Free Credit Report","u":"/client-area-free-credit-report#free-credit-report","p":"client-area-free-credit-report.html","x":"Free Credit Report Before you make a decision about borrowing, you will need to check your credit report. FREE CHECKMYFILE CREDIT REPORT We would recommend you review your personal credit report before you approach lenders, and at regular intervals to ensure it is correct. The reason for this is that once an application is made to a lender they will access your credit file information via one or more of the recognised credit reference agencies, before they commit to making a formal offer to you. It is FREE for 7 days, then £14.99 a month. Cancel anytime Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"Free Credit Report","h":"How credit scores work","u":"/client-area-free-credit-report#how-credit-scores-work","p":"client-area-free-credit-report.html","x":"How credit scores work There are three main credit referencing agencies in the UK, and they will paint a picture of you, by scrutinising all of your personal banking information, to determine how well you manage credit. The information is summarised to produce a single number, which is known as your credit score. Credit scores are considered to be very important, here in the UK, in personal finance, mortgage and commercial lending. As part of your borrowing assessment, we will establish the precise details of your credit history, in order to approach the most suitable lender, based upon your credit score. We believe the best way for you to understand your credit report and score, is to see it for yourself. There are two ways you can achieve this, you can personally request your report from any or all of the big three credit referencing agencies on an individual basis, or you could go through our partners, who use a simple, line-by-line comparison to show you the information held about you by Equifax, Experian and TransUnion, all in one report. Try it FREE for 7 days, then £14.99 a month. Cancel anytime. Once you have a copy of your report, please contact us at Quantock Financial Services to discuss the next steps."},{"t":"Mortgage Calculator","h":"Mortgage Calculator","u":"/client-area-mortgage-calculator#calculator","p":"client-area-mortgage-calculator.html","x":"Mortgage Calculator A quick and easy way to calculate your monthly mortgage payments. Simply enter the amount you wish to borrow, the interest rate and the term over which you intend to pay it off and. Then hit the ‘Calculate’ button. We have extensive mortgage and financial services experience and put our clients’ needs first. Offering a comprehensive choice of mortgages, many that can’t be found on the high street. All our advice is tailor-made to our clients’ requirements. This information is a guide only and should not be relied on as a recommendation or advice that any particular mortgage is suitable for you. All mortgages are subject to the applicant(s) meeting the eligibility criteria of lenders. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. How much could I borrow? Try our borrowing calculator"},{"t":"Borrowing Calculator","h":"How much could I borrow?","u":"/client-area-borrowing-calculator#borrowing-calculator","p":"client-area-borrowing-calculator.html","x":"How much could I borrow? Most lenders will usually lend between 4.5 and 5 times your yearly income before tax. Some lend more to first time buyers, higher earners or certain professions. Add your income, plus a second applicant's if you are buying together, and your deposit to see a rough guide to your budget. to £ - , so a home of up to £ - with your deposit This information is a guide only and should not be relied on as a recommendation or advice that any particular mortgage is suitable for you. All mortgages are subject to the applicant(s) meeting the eligibility criteria of lenders. Your actual amount depends on your outgoings, credit history and each lender’s checks. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. Work out your monthly payments with our mortgage calculator"},{"t":"Stamp Duty Calculator","h":"Stamp Duty","u":"/client-area-stamp-duty-calculator#stamp-duty","p":"client-area-stamp-duty-calculator.html","x":"Stamp Duty You must pay Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT) if you buy a property or land over a certain price in England and Northern Ireland. The tax is different if the property or land is in: Scotland: pay Land and Buildings Transaction Tax Wales: pay Land Transaction Tax if the sale was completed on or after 1 April 2018 Tax treatment is based on individual circumstances and may be subject to change in the future. Information is based on our current understanding of taxation legislation and regulations. Any levels and bases of, and reliefs from taxation, are subject to change. The Financial Conduct Authority does not regulate tax planning. If you wish to seek advice on Stamp Duty, it’s advisable to speak to your solicitor/tax adviser."},{"t":"Stamp Duty Calculator","h":"If you're replacing your main residence","u":"/client-area-stamp-duty-calculator#if-you-re-replacing-your-main-residence","p":"client-area-stamp-duty-calculator.html","x":"If you're replacing your main residence You won’t pay the extra 5% SDLT if the property you’re buying is replacing your main residence and that has already been sold. If there’s a delay selling your main residence and it hasn’t been sold on the day you complete your new purchase: you’ll have to pay higher rates because you own 2 properties you may be able to get a refund if you sell your previous main home within 36 months Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage."},{"t":"Stamp Duty Calculator","h":"Stamp Duty Calculator","u":"/client-area-stamp-duty-calculator#stamp-duty-calculator","p":"client-area-stamp-duty-calculator.html","x":"Stamp Duty Calculator If you would like to work out your SDLT liability, please click the link below www.gov.uk"},{"t":"Contact Us","h":"Make an enquiry","u":"/contact#make-an-enquiry","p":"contact.html","x":"Quantock Financial Services provides mortgage & protection advice for clients throughout Taunton and Somerset. Saving money on monthly outgoings is most people’s priority, and for many people this saving can be made on their mortgage payments. We can offer special deals from our choice of lenders, there is no charge made for advice until the offer of mortgage. Make an enquiry By submitting this information you have given your agreement to receive verbal contact from us to discuss your financial requirements. You voluntarily choose to provide personal details to us via this website. Personal information will be treated as confidential by us and held in accordance with the Data Protection Act 2018."},{"t":"News, Views & Advice","h":"Navigating the mortgage market","u":"/news#navigating-the-mortgage-market","p":"news.html","x":"Newsletter Navigating the mortgage market Pathway: a guided journey toward financial security Volume 1: navigating the mortgage market This month, we’re focusing on […] Shared ownership: Your guide to homebuying on a budget This month, we’re focusing on Shared Ownership and Client Resilience. With mortgage deals changing quickly, understanding your options has […] Protection What is critical illness cover? Whether you need critical illness protection depends on your situation as well as any existing policies you might already have […] Mortgage First-time buyers Being a first-time buyer can be daunting. Not only are you about to make one of the biggest financial decisions […] First-time buyer: Compromise We can all admit that it’s easy to get a little carried away when looking for our ‘dream’ home, but […] First-time buyers: Get the right advice Of course, we’re going to say that: it’s what we do! We’ll review your circumstances and look at your […] First-time buyers: Know your budget Before you start house hunting, it’s essential to know what you can afford. We’ll review your financial circumstances in detail, […]"},{"t":"Navigating the mortgage market","h":"Pathway: a guided journey toward financial security","u":"/news-navigating-the-mortgage-market#pathway-a-guided-journey-toward-financial-securi","p":"news-navigating-the-mortgage-market.html","x":"5 March 2025 Pathway: a guided journey toward financial security Volume 1: navigating the mortgage market This month, we’re focusing on navigating the mortgage market . With mortgage deals changing quickly, understanding your options has never been more important. In this issue, we cover: • The average mortgage shelf life and what it means for borrowers • How a mortgage adviser can help you choose the right deal • Understanding interest rates and their impact on repayments • What lenders look for when approving applications Click below to view our latest newsletter: View the newsletter (PDF)"},{"t":"Shared ownership: Your guide to homebuying on a budget","h":"Click below to view our latest newsletter:","u":"/news-shared-ownership-your-guide-to-homebuying-on-a-budget#click-below-to-view-our-latest-newsletter","p":"news-shared-ownership-your-guide-to-homebuying-on-a-budget.html","x":"22 July 2025 This month, we’re focusing on Shared Ownership and Client Resilience . With mortgage deals changing quickly, understanding your options has never been more important. In this issue, we cover: • What is shared ownership? • Who is shared ownership for? Click below to view our latest newsletter: View the newsletter (PDF)"},{"t":"What is critical illness cover?","h":"What does critical illness cover?","u":"/news-what-is-critical-illness-cover#what-does-critical-illness-cover","p":"news-what-is-critical-illness-cover.html","x":"29 January 2025 Whether you need critical illness protection depends on your situation as well as any existing policies you might already have in place. Critical illness insurance pays out a one-off, lump sum if you’re diagnosed with a condition or disability that is covered by your policy. It can be offered when someone applies for life insurance, as extra coverage. In a similar way to some life insurance plans, critical illness covers a set number of years. You can specify whether you want the payout to rise over the course of the term (so it keeps up with inflation) or the opposite: decreasing because your aim is to cover something specific like your mortgage. If you’re thinking about critical illness cover, it’s important to speak to your financial adviser who can help you decide how much cover you’ll need and how long the term should last. What does critical illness cover? Products vary depending on the provider. Certain illnesses are covered as standard by most insurers, including cancer, heart attack, stroke, organ failure, multiple sclerosis, loss of arms or legs and Alzheimer’s and Parkinson’s disease. Some providers may allow you to add additional illnesses to your policy, which you’ll pay more for. Your children could also be covered as part of your policy so it’s worth asking your adviser about these options if it’s something you’re keen to have in place. What does critical illness not cover? Although a diagnosis of a critical illness can mark the start of a claim in some policies, others may only begin to offer protection once your illness hits a certain level of severity. For example, if you are diagnosed with cancer, payments may only begin when permanent symptoms have been officially diagnosed. Additionally, not all types of cancer are necessarily covered by critical illness protection. It’s important to work with your financial adviser when reviewing a policy and all the small print before you commit to make sure you are sufficiently covered and aware of areas not included. Pre-existing conditions Just like the life insurance application process, critical illness protection requires you to disclose any pre-existing conditions. If you don’t then your policy could be invalid. Your adviser can search the market for a suitable plan, but you’ll probably have to pay more in premiums and there will likely be some extra exclusions. The price you pay will vary, based on things like age, occupation, state of health, lifestyle and how much coverage you need and for how long. Do you need critical illness cover? There are things to consider if you’re worried about being diagnosed with a critical illness and the impact on your income and ability to keep up with bills (which would not be covered by state benefits when you’re unable to work). Your adviser will help you look at the following areas: Your employer’s coverage: is there any paid leave for illness or disability and for how long? Do you have an existing life insurance policy and if so, does it have any illness coverage included? Could you consider income protection insurance as an alternative to critical illness? Do you have sufficient savings and investments you could use in place of critical illness cover? If you want to proceed, it’s important to work with your adviser to see how much protection you’ll need. This means looking at your monthly outgoings and how much you and your family require to live comfortably. You might want to add in any potential costs from medical treatment you may need. During these important decisions it’s easy to lose track of the small details, which is why your adviser can help make the process easier for you and your family and give you some peace of mind. We can examine your needs and existing policies and then find you the right cover that protects your finances (and your family) should anything happen. Contact us about critical illness cover"},{"t":"First-time buyers","h":"Related Posts","u":"/news-first-time-buyers-considerations#related-posts","p":"news-first-time-buyers-considerations.html","x":"29 January 2025 Being a first-time buyer can be daunting. Not only are you about to make one of the biggest financial decisions in your life, but you’ll probably also have family members and friends offering their ideas on the right house, mortgage, lender, conveyancer and even removal company for you. We’ve put together some ideas to try and take away some of the stress and confusion and give you confidence to move through the home buying process as smoothly as possible. Read our related posts for more first-time buyer advice."},{"t":"First-time buyer: Compromise","h":"Related Posts","u":"/news-first-time-buyer-compromise#related-posts","p":"news-first-time-buyer-compromise.html","x":"29 January 2025 We can all admit that it’s easy to get a little carried away when looking for our ‘dream’ home, but sometimes buying your first house is all about compromise. Deciding what you’re prepared to compromise on is an essential step when considering your first home. Whether it’s the luxury of extra bedrooms or a bigger garden, it’s unlikely that you’re going to get everything you want at a price you can afford. That doesn’t mean settling for less. It means prioritising what really matters. Start by making a list of your must-haves, nice-to-haves, and dealbreakers . Think about factors like location, space, commute times, and local amenities. Would you trade a shorter commute for a slightly smaller home? Could a property that needs a little work be a smarter long-term investment than one that’s move-in ready? It’s also worth considering what features can be changed over time and which ones are permanent. A smaller kitchen can be renovated, but you can’t pick up a house and move it closer to your workplace. If the price is right and the essentials are there, some compromises can lead to big rewards in the future. By keeping an open mind and focusing on what’s most important, you’ll find a home that meets your needs now, and one that can grow with you over time. We’re here to help you navigate these decisions so you can make a smart, informed choice that balances practicality with your homeownership dreams."},{"t":"First-time buyers: Get the right advice","h":"Related Posts","u":"/news-first-time-buyers-get-the-right-advice#related-posts","p":"news-first-time-buyers-get-the-right-advice.html","x":"29 January 2025 Of course, we’re going to say that: it’s what we do! We’ll review your circumstances and look at your income, debt, day-to-day outgoings, employment and the size of your deposit, to assess what you can afford to borrow now and in the future. We’ll talk you through the types of mortgages we think are right for you and the lenders who offer them. There are many types of mortgages, and choosing the right one depends on your circumstances. We’ll explain the differences between: Fixed-rate mortgages: ideal for those who prefer stability, as your interest rate stays the same for a set period. Variable-rate mortgages: these can fluctuate, meaning your payments may go up or down. Tracker mortgages: linked to the Bank of England base rate, so your payments move with interest rate changes. Offset mortgages: let you use savings to reduce interest costs, ideal if you have cash reserves. Buying a home comes with additional expenses beyond the mortgage itself, such as stamp duty (if applicable), legal fees, survey costs, and insurance. We’ll help you budget for these so there are no surprises down the line. And your mortgage journey doesn’t end once you secure a deal. We’re here for you in the long run, whether it’s reviewing your mortgage when your fixed term ends, advising on overpayments to reduce your term, or helping if your circumstances change. Buying your first home is an exciting milestone, and we’re here to make it happen. Let’s get started today!"},{"t":"First-time buyers: Know your budget","h":"Related Posts","u":"/news-first-time-buyers-know-your-budget#related-posts","p":"news-first-time-buyers-know-your-budget.html","x":"29 January 2025 Before you start house hunting, it’s essential to know what you can afford. We’ll review your financial circumstances in detail, considering your income, existing debts, day-to-day spending, employment status, and the size of your deposit. This helps us determine a realistic borrowing amount, ensuring you don’t overstretch yourself financially. Your hard-saved deposit and monthly mortgage repayments aren’t the only expenses you need to be mindful of when buying your first home: Some lenders will charge a valuation fee to help them establish how much they are prepared to lend you. You’ll also need to factor in the cost of a survey (depending on the type of property you’re buying and the lender you choose to go with, you might need a basic mortgage valuation, a homebuyers report or a full structural survey). In Scotland you also need to budget for Land and Buildings Transaction Tax on properties over £175,000. In Wales you’ll need to budget for Land Transaction Tax on properties over £225,000. In England or Northern Ireland, you’ll need to budget for Stamp Duty Land Tax on properties over £300,000. You’ll also need to pay your solicitor or conveyancer for any legal work and local searches they do on your behalf."},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Quantock Financial is a trading name of Quantock Financial Services Ltd which is an appointed representative of The Openwork Partnership, a trading style of Openwork Limited which is authorised and regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority."},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Quantock Financial Services Ltd Registered Office: Quantock Financial Services Ltd, Quantock Financial 1 The Crescent, Taunton, Somerset, TA1 4EA. Registered company number 12383613. Registered in England & Wales."},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"The guidance and/or advice contained within the website is subject to the UK regulatory regime and is therefore primarily targeted at customers in the UK."},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Our alternative dispute resolution provider is the Financial Ombudsman Service. Their website is financial-ombudsman.org.uk"},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Please read our privacy statement before making an enquiry. Our Address: Quantock Financial, 1 The Crescent, Taunton, Somerset, TA1 4EA. Approved by The Openwork Partnership on 15.05.24"},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on your mortgage. You may have to pay an early repayment charge to your existing lender if you remortgage. As with all insurance policies, conditions and exclusions will apply. There may be a fee for arranging a Mortgage and the precise amount will depend on your circumstances. This will typically be £399. Not all Buy To Let Mortgages are regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority.","k":"cost costs fee fees price charge charges how much pay advice broker arranging"},{"t":"Important information","h":"Important information","u":"/#footer","p":"index.html","x":"Think carefully before securing other debts against your home. Your home may be repossessed if you do not keep up repayments on a mortgage or loan secured against it. The Financial Conduct Authority does not regulate Commercial Lending or some forms of Buy-to-Let."},{"t":"Contact Us","h":"Contact us","u":"/contact","p":"contact.html","x":"Quantock Financial, 1 The Crescent, Taunton, Somerset, TA1 4EA. Call us on 01823 426046, WhatsApp us or email office@quantockfinancial.co.uk.","k":"contact address where based located office find visit location phone telephone number call email whatsapp ring speak"}];